      {
        "title": "2. Engine — stubbed logic (write test-first, ready for when each lands)",
        "content": "\nFor each, I'm giving the scenarios that follow directly from the docstring/contract already committed, so the tests double as an executable spec.\n\n**`pipeline/normalize.py::fold_name` / `normalize`** — case/whitespace/punctuation folding, legal-suffix stripping (`\"Acme, Inc.\"` → `\"acme\"`), folding is *recorded not destructive* (`name` unchanged, only `normalized_name` folded), idempotence (`fold_name(fold_name(x)) == fold_name(x)`), unicode/accents, empty string, suffix that's also a real word (e.g. an account literally named `\"Co\"`).\n```python\ndef test_fold_name_strips_legal_suffix_and_case():\n    assert fold_name(\"Acme, Inc.\") == \"acme\"\n\ndef test_fold_name_is_idempotent():\n    assert fold_name(fold_name(\"Acme Corp.\")) == fold_name(\"Acme Corp.\")\n\ndef test_normalize_preserves_original_name_field():\n    [entity] = normalize([{\"id\": \"1\", \"Name\": \"Acme Inc.\"}], entity_type=\"account\", source_id=\"s1\")\n    assert entity.name == \"Acme Inc.\"\n    assert entity.normalized_name == \"acme\"\n```\n\n**`pipeline/blocking.py::block/score/combine/match`** — exact-key match takes priority over embedding (a pair that matches on external id should never also appear via FAISS); `top_k` is respected; **O(n·k) invariant**: assert candidate pair count for N=1000 entities stays bounded regardless of N² blowup (this is a stated hard requirement, not a nice-to-have); `combine` weights sum/behave sanely at signal extremes (all-agree → confidence ≈1, all-disagree → confidence ≈0); confidence is clamped to `[0,1]`.\n```python\ndef test_blocking_never_compares_all_pairs():\n    side_a = [make_entity(f\"a{i}\") for i in range(500)]\n    side_b = [make_entity(f\"b{i}\") for i in range(500)]\n    pairs = block(side_a, side_b, top_k=10)\n    assert len(pairs) <= 500 * 10  # not 500*500\n\ndef test_exact_key_match_short_circuits_embedding_scoring():\n    a = make_entity(\"acc-1\", external_id=\"X\")\n    b = make_entity(\"acc-1-dup\", external_id=\"X\")\n    [m] = match([a], [b], run_id=RUN_ID)\n    assert m.method == \"exact_key\"\n```\n\n**`pipeline/detect.py::classify/detect`** — this is the severity model from AGENTS.md and deserves a full parametrized matrix, plus duplicate-is-always-4, plus orphan wiring to `severity_for_orphan`.\n```python\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"field,va,vb,expected_class,expected_severity\", [\n    (\"parentExternalId\", \"ACC-1\", \"ACC-2\", \"structural\", 4),\n    (\"territory\", \"APAC\", \"EMEA\", \"attribute\", 3),\n    (\"industry\", \"Tech\", \"Software\", \"attribute\", 2),\n    (\"name\", \"Acme Inc.\", \"ACME INC\", \"cosmetic\", 1),\n])\ndef test_classify_matrix(field, va, vb, expected_class, expected_severity):\n    assert classify(field, va, vb) == (expected_class, expected_severity)\n\ndef test_duplicate_record_is_always_severity_4():\n    # two side-A records matching one side-B record\n    conflicts = detect(two_to_one_matches, entities, run_id=RUN_ID)\n    dup = next(c for c in conflicts if c.conflict_class == \"duplicate\")\n    assert dup.severity == 4\n```\n\n**`agent/graph.py::select_rule/validate/resolve`** — the two non-negotiable invariants from AGENTS.md deserve explicit tests: (1) most-specific-first with **stable tie-break on author order**, (2) `validate` rejecting any value that isn't one of the two observed values or \"a legal normalization\" of one — this is the hallucination guard and should be adversarially tested with a fabricated value.\n```python\ndef test_select_rule_prefers_most_specific():\n    wildcard = make_rule(field=\"*\", entity_type=\"*\")\n    specific = make_rule(field=\"territory\", entity_type=\"account\")\n    assert select_rule(territory_conflict, [wildcard, specific]) == specific\n\ndef test_select_rule_ties_keep_author_order():\n    first = make_rule(field=\"territory\", entity_type=\"account\", id=\"r1\")\n    second = make_rule(field=\"territory\", entity_type=\"account\", id=\"r2\")  # same specificity\n    assert select_rule(territory_conflict, [first, second]).id == \"r1\"\n\ndef test_no_matching_rule_escalates():\n    assert select_rule(territory_conflict, []) is None\n\ndef test_validate_accepts_observed_values():\n    assert validate(\"APAC-SOUTH\", value_a=\"APAC-SOUTH\", value_b=\"APAC\")\n\ndef test_validate_rejects_hallucinated_value():\n    # NON-NEGOTIABLE per AGENTS.md: a value the model invented must fail.\n    assert not validate(\"APAC-CENTRAL\", value_a=\"APAC-SOUTH\", value_b=\"APAC\")\n\ndef test_validate_accepts_legal_normalization_of_observed_value():\n    assert validate(\"acme\", value_a=\"Acme Inc.\", value_b=\"ACME INC\")\n```\n\n**`agent/prompts.py::build_ruleset_block/build_conflict_block`** — the prompt-caching cost lever depends on byte-identical prefixes; that's directly testable without calling the model.\n```python\ndef test_ruleset_block_is_byte_identical_across_calls():\n    assert build_ruleset_block(rules) == build_ruleset_block(rules)\n\ndef test_ruleset_block_unaffected_by_conflict_content():\n    # varying-per-call data must never leak into the cached prefix\n    block1 = build_ruleset_block(rules)\n    build_conflict_block(conflict_a)\n    block2 = build_ruleset_block(rules)\n    assert block1 == block2\n```\n\n**`agent/tools.py::get_entity_history/get_children`** — read-only-by-construction; test that these never issue writes (spy/mock any DB or store used) and return `[]` for an unknown `external_id` rather than raising.\n\n**`connectors/synthetic.py`, `salesforce.py`, `warehouse.py`** — `SyntheticConnector.extract` should stream (test it's a generator, not eagerly materialized — feed it a huge fixture and assert memory isn't the bottleneck via `itertools.islice` without exhausting); malformed row in the source file should raise a clear error, not a silent skip. Salesforce/Warehouse are Phase 5 — flag now that they'll need **credential-isolation tests**: assert `sources.config` passed to the constructor never contains keys that look like secrets (`token`, `password`, `secret`), which is cheap to write now and guards the \"never `sources.config`\" rule from day one.\n\n**`db.py`** (all functions) — every function here needs an **integration test against a real/test Postgres**, not a unit test, since it's pure I/O. Priority scenarios: `update_run_status` never touches `sourceAId`/`sourceBId`/`rulesetId` columns; `append_audit` is genuinely append-only (attempt an UPDATE via the same connection after insert and confirm the module exposes no path to it); `write_entities`/`write_matches`/`write_conflicts` reject writing to `sources`/`rulesets` tables (this should be impossible by construction — a test that tries to misuse the module and gets a `TypeError`/`AttributeError` is the right shape, since there's no `write_sources` function to even call).\n\n**`main.py`** — FastAPI TestClient tests: `POST /runs` returns 202-shaped `{runId, accepted:true}` immediately without blocking on `execute_run` (assert response latency doesn't scale with dataset size — use a mocked slow `execute_run`); `GET /runs/{id}/status` for an unknown UUID (404, not 500); `execute_run` partial-failure behavior — AGENTS.md explicitly requires \"a run that fails halfway leaves a readable trail rather than an empty row,\" which means a test that kills `execute_run` mid-pipeline (e.g. mock `detect` to raise) and asserts `runs.status` was already advanced past `\"matching\"` before the failure, plus `runs.error` is populated.\n\n**`eval/generate.py::generate`** — the most important stub in the repo per AGENTS.md (\"build this before the engine\"). Scenarios: same `--seed` → byte-identical output across two runs (reproducibility is the whole point); every `InjectionRates` category actually appears in the manifest at roughly the configured rate (statistical assertion with tolerance, large N); the `ambiguous` category specifically produces entries whose `expected_resolution == \"escalate\"`; hierarchy shape is non-uniform (assert stdev of subtree sizes > 0, not a uniform tree).\n```python\ndef test_same_seed_is_reproducible():\n    m1 = generate(seed=42, accounts=100, out_dir=tmp_path_a)\n    m2 = generate(seed=42, accounts=100, out_dir=tmp_path_b)\n    assert m1 == m2\n\ndef test_ambiguous_entries_expect_escalation():\n    manifest = generate(seed=1, accounts=200, out_dir=tmp_path)\n    ambiguous = [e for e in manifest if e.conflict_class == \"duplicate\" or \"ambiguous\" in e.entity_id]\n    assert all(e.expected_resolution == \"escalate\" for e in ambiguous)\n```\n\n**`eval/harness.py`, `baselines.py`, `report.py`** — `run_system` for all three `System` values against a tiny fixed manifest with hand-computed expected precision/recall (a golden-number test, not just \"doesn't crash\"); `sweep_auto_apply_threshold` — monotonicity check (raising the threshold should never *increase* false-auto-apply rate); `ablate_without_validate` should demonstrably produce a nonzero invented-value rate on an adversarial fixture (this is the empirical proof the AGENTS.md invariant #2 needs — right now nothing enforces that the ablation actually shows a difference); `baseline_rules` vs `baseline_exact` on the same manifest — `baseline_exact` should always have recall ≥ `baseline_rules`... actually inverse — worth explicitly asserting the ordering AGENTS.md implies (\"is the agent earning its cost?\") once `canon_full` exists too.\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "3. Web — implemented, untested",
        "content": "\n",
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "3.1 `lib/cn.ts::cn`",
        "content": "```ts\n// web/lib/cn.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { cn } from \"@/lib/cn\";\n\ndescribe(\"cn\", () => {\n  it(\"merges conflicting tailwind classes, last one wins\", () => {\n    expect(cn(\"px-2\", \"px-4\")).toBe(\"px-4\");\n  });\n  it(\"drops falsy values\", () => {\n    expect(cn(\"a\", false, undefined, null, \"b\")).toBe(\"a b\");\n  });\n  it(\"handles empty input\", () => {\n    expect(cn()).toBe(\"\");\n  });\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "3.2 Zod schemas — `types/source.ts`, `types/rules.ts`, `types/conflict.ts`, `types/resolution.ts`, `types/run.ts`",
        "content": "These are the actual runtime contract enforcement (every route handler validates with these) and currently have zero coverage. Highest-value gaps: the `refine` on `survivorshipRuleSchema` (mirrors the Python validator — needs a **parity test**, see §4), the discriminated union on `reviewActionSchema`, and completeness of the label maps against their enums (a new `SourceKind` added to the union without updating `SOURCE_KIND_LABEL` currently fails silently at render time, not at build/test time).\n```ts\n// web/types/rules.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { survivorshipRuleSchema, ruleSpecificity } from \"@/types/rules\";\n\ndescribe(\"survivorshipRuleSchema\", () => {\n  it(\"rejects prefer_source without preferredSourceId\", () => {\n    const result = survivorshipRuleSchema.safeParse({\n      id: \"r1\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"prefer_source\", description: \"d\",\n    });\n    expect(result.success).toBe(false);\n  });\n\n  it(\"accepts prefer_source with preferredSourceId\", () => {\n    const result = survivorshipRuleSchema.safeParse({\n      id: \"r1\", field: \"*\", entityType: \"*\", strategy: \"prefer_source\",\n      preferredSourceId: \"src-a\", description: \"d\",\n    });\n    expect(result.success).toBe(true);\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"ruleSpecificity\", () => {\n  it(\"scores exact field + exact entityType highest\", () => {\n    const rule = { id: \"r1\", field: \"territory\", entityType: \"account\", strategy: \"escalate\", description: \"d\" };\n    expect(ruleSpecificity(rule)).toBe(3);\n  });\n});\n\n// web/types/source.test.ts\nimport { SOURCE_KIND_LABEL, SOURCE_KIND_SIDE, sourceKindSchema } from \"@/types/source\";\n\nit(\"every SourceKind enum member has a label and a side\", () => {\n  for (const kind of sourceKindSchema.options) {\n    expect(SOURCE_KIND_LABEL[kind]).toBeDefined();\n    expect(SOURCE_KIND_SIDE[kind]).toBeDefined();\n  }\n});\n\nit(\"sourceConfigSchema rejects an empty objects array\", () => {\n  expect(sourceConfigSchema.safeParse({ objects: [] }).success).toBe(false);\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "3.3 `lib/server/sources.ts::listSources` / `countSourcesBySide`",
        "content": "Needs a DB mock (drizzle supports an in-memory/mock db, or mock `getDb`). **Real gap found while reading this**: `countSourcesBySide` buckets by explicit `kind === \"salesforce\" || kind === \"hubspot\"` etc. — a `SourceKind` added later that isn't wired into one of the three branches silently disappears from all counts with no error. Worth a completeness test that asserts every kind lands in exactly one bucket.\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/sources.test.ts\nimport { describe, it, expect, vi } from \"vitest\";\nimport { countSourcesBySide } from \"@/lib/server/sources\";\n\nit(\"every SourceKind is counted in exactly one bucket\", async () => {\n  // stub listSources() to return one source per kind, then assert\n  // crm + warehouse + synthetic counts sum to the total source count.\n});\n\nit(\"returns zero counts when there are no sources\", async () => {\n  const counts = await countSourcesBySide(); // with listSources mocked to []\n  expect(counts).toEqual({ crm: 0, warehouse: 0, synthetic: 0 });\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "3.4 `lib/db/index.ts::getDb`",
        "content": "Error-path gap: no test asserts the actionable error message when `DATABASE_URL` is unset — this is the one deliberately-authored error path in the file and it's uncovered.\n```ts\nit(\"throws an actionable error when DATABASE_URL is unset\", () => {\n  delete process.env.DATABASE_URL;\n  expect(() => getDb()).toThrow(/DATABASE_URL is not set/);\n});\n```\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "3.5 Components — presentational, but one design invariant is testable and worth locking in",
        "content": "AGENTS.md is explicit: *\"Never render the [severity] mark without the word.\"* That's a hard product invariant, not decoration — a regression here breaks accessibility for the whole console.\n```tsx\n// web/components/severity-mark.test.tsx\nimport { render, screen } from \"@testing-library/react\";\nimport { SeverityMark } from \"@/components/severity-mark\";\n\nit.each([1, 2, 3, 4] as const)(\"severity %i always renders its text label, never mark alone\", (severity) => {\n  render(<SeverityMark severity={severity} />);\n  expect(screen.getByText(/Structural|Attribute|Cosmetic/)).toBeInTheDocument();\n});\n```\n`SourceTable`/`SourcesEmptyState`/`PageHeader` and the landing components are pure render-from-props — lower priority; a smoke render test per component (renders without throwing, given valid props) is enough until they grow interaction logic.\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 3
      },
      {
        "title": "4. Integration / cross-layer gaps",
        "content": "\nThese are the ones unit tests structurally can't catch, and none currently exist:\n\n1. **Zod ↔ Pydantic contract parity.** Every enum/shape in `engine/pipeline/schema.py` is hand-duplicated in `web/types/`. Nothing currently proves they agree. Suggest a fixture-based parity test: a shared JSON fixture file that both a Python test (`pytest`, parses with the Pydantic model) and a TS test (`vitest`, parses with the Zod schema) must accept identically — run in CI on both sides whenever either `schema.py` or `web/types/*.ts` changes.\n2. **Table-ownership enforcement.** AGENTS.md states the engine must never write `sources`/`rulesets` and the web layer must never write `entities`/`matches`/`conflicts`/`resolutions`(engine-proposed columns). Nothing enforces this beyond \"don't write that function.\" Once `db.py` is implemented, add a Postgres-role-based integration test: connect as the engine's DB role and assert an `INSERT INTO sources` is rejected by grants, not just by convention.\n3. **Full run lifecycle.** `queued → extracting → matching → detecting → resolving → complete`, with `runs.stats` populated at each transition — no test once `execute_run`/`main.py` land. Include the failure path (AGENTS.md: a failed run must leave a readable trail).\n4. **Severity gate end-to-end.** A synthetic conflict with `severity=4` must never reach `status=\"auto_applied\"` even when the model returns `confidence=1.0` — this spans `detect.classify` + `graph.should_auto_apply` + `db.write_resolutions` and is exactly the property the paper's headline metric depends on.\n5. **Audit log append-only, in practice.** Every reviewer action (`approve`/`reject`/`override`) must append, never mutate prior rows — needs a route-handler-level test once `app/api/conflicts/[conflictId]/resolution/route.ts` exists (it doesn't yet — Phase 4 gap, flagged for when it lands).\n6. **Ruleset immutability.** `POST /rulesets` must always create a new version row (Phase 4, not yet built) — flag now so the first implementation ships with the test, not after.\n\n---\n\n",
        "level": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Priority order",
        "content": "1. Install a web test runner (needs your go-ahead per AGENTS.md's dependency policy) and add a `pytest` `tests/` tree in engine — both are currently zero-infrastructure.\n2. §1 (engine implemented-but-untested: `should_auto_apply`, `severity_for_orphan`, `rule_specificity`, the Pydantic contract) and §3 (web implemented-but-untested) — these are real code, testable today, and #1 in particular gates the paper's central claim.\n3. §2 stub skeletons — land alongside each function per AGENTS.md's \"build feature by feature,\" not in a batch at the end.\n4. §4 integration/contract tests — highest leverage per test written, but blocked on Phase 4 (route handlers) and a test Postgres instance.",
